  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe continued dominance of low to mid level anticyclonic flow across \nLower Michigan will provide another dry day across the forecast \narea. After a relatively cool morning, daytime heating will again \nboost afternoon highs into the upper 70s/near 80. Aside from a few \ncu, some cirrus may continue to stream into the area from the west. \nOtherwise, ample sunshine will prevail within the dry ambient \nairmass. The mid level anticyclone and associated surface high will \nbe driven across the eastern Great Lakes Tuesday as an expansive \nregion of mid level height falls encompass the northern Plains and \nupper Midwest. Southerly return flow will advect a slightly warmer \nand more humid airmass into southern Michigan. Model guidance is \nstrongly supportive of highs into the low 80s Tuesday. The moisture \nadvection may allow some increase in cloud cover during the day \nwhile a strong capping inversion inhibits convection. \n\nThe aforementioned height falls will be associated with a \nprogressive mid level low moving from the Canadian high plains \nTuesday into northern Ontario Wednesday. This system will send a \ncold front into Lower Mi Wednesday and will flatten the large ridge \nacross the Conus. This will place the Great Lakes region within \nstronger mid/upper level westerlies. \n\nOf note within the last few runs of the model suite has been a plume \nof deep layer moisture circulating around the large ridge and \nadvecting into Lower Mi Tues night/Wednesday in advance of the cold \nfront. ECMWF ensemble members indicate precipitable water values \nranging anywhere from 1.8 to 2.2 inches within this moist axis, quite\nhigh for anytime of the year in the Michigan. The advection of the \ndeep layer moisture plume into Lower Mi Tuesday night will warrant a \nchance of convection, especially across the northern portions of the \nforecast area. Conditions will be favorable for upscale convective \ngrowth to the west/southwest of Lower Mi Tuesday night. Downstream \npropagation of any remnant convection/MCV(s) into Lower Mi Wednesday \nwill warrant a chance of showers/thunderstorms. Residual cloud cover\nand precip leads to some uncertainty as to the advection of \ninstability into Se Mi Wednesday. While the operational NAM is quite \nbullish on instability, the majority of model solutions and their \nensemble members continue to hint at overall weak instability across \nSe Mi, with the axis of higher daytime instability remaining south of\nthe state. With the low level frontal boundary and moist axis \naligning more parallel to the mean flow Wednesday, even weak \nconvection may support some prolonged rain and thus locally high rain\ntotals. More notable short wave energy forecast to traverse the \nnorthern lakes late Wed/Wed night is forecast to drive the frontal \nsystem into the Ohio Valley, bringing a little cooler and drier \nairmass to forecast area for the later portion of the work week.",
